WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.037
Sometimes an outer condition makes an inner conditional relationship hold.

2
00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:09.028
The inner if-clause is part of the result of that outer condition.

3
00:00:09.328 --> 00:00:14.299
If the trial is funded, then if its protocol is approved, the trial is registered.

4
00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:19.228
Funding supplies a relationship: approval would be enough for registration.

5
00:00:19.528 --> 00:00:23.325
Funding alone does not establish approval or registration.

6
00:00:23.625 --> 00:00:27.465
If both funding and approval are given, registration follows.

7
00:00:27.765 --> 00:00:37.856
The same statement can be written with a combined sufficient side, funded AND approved → registered, but the grouping helps us see where that combination came from.

8
00:00:38.156 --> 00:00:42.828
An extra if can make it tempting to treat the inner sufficient condition as a fact.

9
00:00:43.128 --> 00:00:46.243
Separating outer and inner scope prevents that mistake.

10
00:00:46.543 --> 00:00:50.361
Keep the entire inner conditional together as the outer result.

11
00:00:50.661 --> 00:00:53.797
It is a rule at this point, not an asserted inner event.

12
00:00:54.097 --> 00:00:56.807
Use brackets when they make the scope easier to read.

13
00:00:57.107 --> 00:01:03.763
Do not flatten a conditional result into a fact or treat the final result as proof of the outer condition.

14
00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:08.265
This extension is optional after the core compound and chaining work.
